26.4  Logging from your own programs and services

TL;DR
  - Under systemd, a service only has to print to standard output or error:
    journald stores each line with the time, unit and PID.
  - Prefix a line with <3> (error) to <7> (debug) to set its priority;
    journalctl -p can then filter it.
  - From a shell script, logger sends a message to the journal; systemd-cat
    sends a whole command's output.

      logger -p user.err "backup failed: disk full"
      systemd-cat -t backup ./backup.sh
      journalctl -t backup                  # by tag

Pitfalls
  - Writing your own log file from a service means handling rotation,
    permissions and disk space yourself: standard output is simpler.
  - Never log secrets: passwords, tokens and full request bodies end up in files
    many people and tools can read.
  - Logging every request at full detail can cost more than the work: keep debug
    logs off in production, behind a level setting.
  - Python's output to a pipe is buffered: set PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1, or lines
    reach the journal late.

Deep dive

  ▾ Plain text or structured logs?

    Plain lines are easy to read and grep. Structured logs (JSON, or the
    journal's own fields) are easy to filter and count: user_id=42,
    duration_ms=180. Many services print JSON for a log collector to index; the
    journal can also take extra fields directly through its API.

  ▾ Which level for what?

    Error: something failed and someone should look. Warning: unexpected but
    handled. Info: what happened, at a level an operator follows. Debug: details
    for the developer, off by default. If every request logs a warning, warnings
    stop meaning anything.

